4. Controller Manager

This one is very important.
The Controller Manager continuously checks whether the actual cluster matches what you requested.

Suppose you requested:  replicas = 3
You want:  3 Pods

Initially:
Pod 1 → Running
Pod 2 → Running
Pod 3 → Running

Everything is good.

Now imagine:  Pod 2 → crashes/deleted

You now have:
Pod 1 → Running
Pod 2 → ❌
Pod 3 → Running

But your desired state is:  3 Pods
The Controller Manager notices:
Desired = 3
Actual  = 2

So it takes action to get back to:
Desired = 3
Actual  = 3

It causes the appropriate controller, such as the Deployment/ReplicaSet controllers, to create a replacement Pod.


Complete Example
Let's put everything together.

You run:  kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx --replicas=3

Step 1 — kubectl → API Server
kubectl
   |
   | HTTPS :6443
   ↓
API Server

API Server receives your request.

Step 2 — API Server → etcd
The cluster state is recorded through the API Server.

Conceptually:
API Server
    |
    ↓
  etcd

Deployment nginx
replicas = 3

Now Kubernetes knows:  "The user wants 3 nginx Pods."

Step 3 — Controller Manager
The Deployment/ReplicaSet controllers see that the desired state requires Pods.

Desired:
3 nginx Pods

Actual:
0 nginx Pods

So the controllers work toward creating the required Pod objects.

Step 4 — Scheduler
The Pods need nodes.

Scheduler sees unscheduled Pods:
nginx-pod-1 → no node
nginx-pod-2 → no node
nginx-pod-3 → no node

It chooses suitable nodes:
nginx-pod-1 → Node 1
nginx-pod-2 → Node 2
nginx-pod-3 → Node 3

Step 5 — Worker Node
Now the selected Worker Nodes have to actually run the Pods.
The kubelet on each selected node communicates with the API Server and makes sure the assigned Pod is running.
API Server
    |
    | Pod assignment
    ↓
kubelet on Node 1
    |
    ↓
Container Runtime
    |
    ↓
nginx container

Same thing on Node 2 and Node 3.

Finally:
Node 1        Node 2        Node 3
  |             |             |
 nginx         nginx         nginx
 Pod 1         Pod 2         Pod 3
Now imagine Pod 2 dies

This is where the architecture becomes easier to understand.
Node 1          Node 2          Node 3
  |               |               |
 Pod 1          ❌ Pod 2         Pod 3

Controller sees:
Desired = 3
Actual  = 2

It wants:
Desired = 3
Actual  = 3

So a replacement Pod is created.
Scheduler can then select an appropriate node for that replacement Pod.

Node 1          Node 2          Node 3
  |               |               |
 Pod 1         New Pod           Pod 3
The easiest way to remember all 4

Think of Kubernetes like a company:

API Server
   ↓
"Front desk"
Receives requests
etcd
   ↓
"Database"
Stores cluster information
Scheduler
   ↓
"Placement manager"
Decides which Worker Node gets a Pod
Controller Manager
   ↓
"Supervisor"
Continuously works toward the desired state
Full picture
                    USER
                     |
                     | kubectl
                     ↓
              ┌───────────────┐
              │   API SERVER  │ :6443
              └───────┬───────┘
                      |
             ┌────────┴─────────┐
             ↓                  ↓
           etcd             Controllers
        (database)          (desired state)
                                |
                                ↓
                           Scheduler
                       (choose Worker Node)
                                |
              ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
              ↓                 ↓                 ↓
           Worker 1          Worker 2          Worker 3
              |                 |                 |
           kubelet           kubelet           kubelet
              |                 |                 |
             Pod               Pod               Pod
              |                 |                 |
          Container         Container         Container

One key correction to keep in mind: the Scheduler chooses the node, while the kubelet on that node actually makes the Pod run.
The Controller Manager keeps checking/reconciling the desired state.

